## Why This Is Asked

Examines practical handling of delayed supervision, distribution drift, and safe deployment in production ML systems.

## Key Concepts

- Delayed feedback and label latency
- Concept drift and drift detectors (ADWIN-like)
- Online vs offline retraining
- Calibration and evaluation (AUROC, PR-AUC, FPR)
- Data/versioning and deployment safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
let model = initModel();
let buffer = [];
stream.on('event', async (e) => {
  const x = featuresFromEvent(e);
  const yHat = model.predict(x);
  storePrediction(e.id, yHat);
  buffer.push({id: e.id, x, timestamp: Date.now()});
});

// Drift detection
setInterval(() => {
  const recent = getRecentPredictions(window);
  const metrics = evaluateMetrics(recent);
  if (driftDetector.detect(metrics)) {
    triggerOfflineRetrain();
  }
}, 3600000);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests on-device constraints, lightweight models, quantization, calibration, drift detection, and retraining cadence. Demonstrates integration of hardware constraints with ML basics.

## Key Concepts
- Edge ML constraints, memory, power
- Model compression (8-bit quantization, pruning)
- Feature hashing vs dense input
- Calibration and drift monitoring
- Data splits for on-device eval

## Code Example
```javascript
// 8-bit quantization sketch
function quantizeWeights(weights){
  const maxAbs = Math.max(...weights.map(Math.abs), 1);
  const scale = 127 / maxAbs;
  return weights.map(w => Math.round(w * scale));
}
```

